WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:08.080
BRENNAN: Hello, everyone. Good morning. I
wanted to thank everyone for coming here today.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.400
It is a great way to start off your morning,
and hopefully we'll have a great conversation.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.020
We're going to have some remarks from the
senator here.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:21.080
Before we get underway, I want to remind everyone
that the conversation is on the record. It

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.810
is being broadcast as well, and we will give
you all a chance – hopefully all – to

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:30.350
ask some questions after we finish the question
and answer period up here -- we'll have some

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.629
microphones for you. I'm sure you've all seen
these and been to these before, so you're

00:00:33.629 --> 00:00:35.890
old hands at that.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:41.489
But this is, of course, Angus King, who's
going to give us a sense of why he wants to

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:47.510
talk right now about Iran, this nuclear deal
that has been signed, and his concern about

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:51.410
implementation and follow through – some
challenges to it.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:56.140
I'm Margaret Brennan from CBS News – I'm
also a term member here at the Council on

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.769
Foreign Relations. So I am excited to be doing
this conversation – my first one as a member.

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:06.890
But I cover foreign policy for CBS News and
national security, so you'll see me on the

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.570
road often in places like Geneva, with deals
like this being negotiated.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.930
But without further ado, just to introduce
the conversation once again, I want to ask

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:24.090
the senator to give us a sense of his thoughts,
and why we're here today. Angus King, of course,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:29.110
an independent from Maine, on armed services,
on intelligence, and has a lot to say about

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.110
this.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.110
KING: Great. Thanks, Margaret.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:35.400
Margaret didn't tell you, but she just got
back from Geneva, so I appreciate your joining

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.330
us today.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:42.140
What I'd like to do, before we get to, hopefully,
a lot of questions -- and this is a story

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:49.240
that's changing by the day -- is first start
with a little bit about who am I, since this

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:56.240
is my first visit with this -- with this group.
The first thing, in terms of where we are,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.950
is that my home town is Alexandria, Virginia.
Although I'm a senator from Maine, I grew

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:08.360
up in Alexandria. My granddad was the mayor
of Alexandria in the '20s. And I went to Maine

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:16.510
in 1969 out of Virginia Law School to join
Lyndon Johnson's War on Poverty. I was with

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:22.240
the -- remember -- some of you remember the
OEO and the Legal Services Corporation, and

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.980
I was assigned to Maine.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.840
Being from Virginia, when I got to Maine,
I had a son born in 1970 in the Skowhegan,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:38.630
Maine, hospital, and I learned at that time
how hard it is to be from Maine because after

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:44.790
my son was born, I was so excited, I went
out on the street and said, Oh -- I ran into

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:49.760
an old farmer friend of mine, Charles Forbus
(ph). I said, Mr. Forbus (ph), guess what?

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.460
I have a son just born in the Skowhegan hospital!
He's a native of Maine!

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:59.190
And the old farmer looked at me over his glasses
and says, Well, by gorry (ph), just because

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:03.200
the cat has her kittens in the oven don't
make 'em biscuits.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.610
(LAUGHTER)

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:10.750
So I'd learned at that moment that you can't
be from Maine even if you've been born there.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.260
Went to Dartmouth College -- by the way, there
are four senators from Dartmouth in the United

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:20.190
States Senate right now, I think the largest
delegation from any one college -- Rob Portman,

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:25.400
Kirsten Gillibrand, John Hoeven and myself.
University of Virginia Law School -- we have

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.130
three senators from Virginia Law School, Sheldon
Whitehouse and Bill Nelson, who was there

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:36.310
when I was. Then went to Maine, as I said,
worked for Pinetree Legal Assistance in rural

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.310
Maine.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:41.630
Then had an interesting experience. This is
relevant to what I'm doing now. I came back

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:46.900
to Washington for two-and-a-half years and
worked as a staff member in the U.S. Senate.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.290
I was sworn in as a United States senator
40 years to the day from the day I entered

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:58.980
service as a staff member of the Senate in
January of 1973. So that's a rich area of

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:05.640
discussion in itself, is what the Senate -- how
the Senate and the Congress has changed during

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.220
that period. It's really fascinating.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.660
For example, I remember going to conference
committees pretty regularly in those days.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:18.320
Now a conference committee -- I think there
were two or maybe three in the last year.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.060
Practiced law in Maine, went into business,
was in the renewable energy business really

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:31.890
for most of my adult life -- hydropower, biomass,
energy conservation, wind power -- and spent

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:36.270
a lot of time in the energy business. And
then was governor of Maine for eight years

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:42.180
in the '90s and into 2002 as an independent.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.910
And then retired from politics, taught at
Bowdoin and Bates Colleges, worked on some

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:53.669
boards, did a variety of things, had no intention
or expectation of ever getting back into politics

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:59.600
or ever running for anything. And then in
February of 2012, Olympia Snowe, to everyone's

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.580
shock and surprise, announced her retirement,
basically because, she said, I'm just sick

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.220
and tired of the Senate and the Congress not
working.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:14.639
And I thought, Well, maybe I can make a contribution
as an independent and try to make the institution

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:19.230
work a little bit better. So that was the
-- that was the beginning of my reentry into

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:29.181
politics in the spring of 2012. I was elected
in November of 2012 and started in the Senate

00:05:29.181 --> 00:05:36.470
last year as the world's oldest freshman and
was appointed -- of course, you know, I -- first,

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:41.180
my intention was to be a pure independent.
Chris Matthews -- I was on "Hardball." Chris

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:46.320
said -- by the way, if you're ever -- when
you're with Chris, he interviews himself,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.450
really.

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:48.590
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:52.210
And you're there -- you're sort of the wallpaper.
But he said, Angus, why don't you just put

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.639
your chair in the middle and be a real independent?
Don't join any party's caucus and everything.

00:05:56.639 --> 00:06:01.990
Well, that was fine until I found out you
don't get committee assignments except through

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:03.229
the caucus.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:11.760
So I joined the Democratic caucus and was
appointed to Armed Services, Rules, Budget,

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.340
and to prove Harry Reid has a sense of humor,
Intelligence. And those are the committees,

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:22.010
ironically, when ended up putting me right
in the center of everything that went on last

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:31.550
year. Syria, the budget, Iran, NSA, Snowden
-- all of those issues, I've been up to my

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:35.900
elbows in right from the very beginning.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:50.030
And that brings us to today, which -- I wanted
to talk for a few minutes about Iran. The

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:58.940
story is changing literally as we sit here.
This morning, there's a Reuters report that

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:05.479
the advocates for the new sanctions bill have
now -- are talking about backing off and having

00:07:05.479 --> 00:07:12.460
another kind of bill but not necessarily new
sanctions. So all my carefully written objections

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:17.280
to why we shouldn't do sanctions may be moot
as of this morning.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.790
But I want to talk generally about the situation
and about the sanctions bill because, you

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:29.870
know, things can change on a sort of day-to-day
basis. Just before the break last week, I

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:35.580
talked to one of the senior senators about
this issue, and his comment was, Well, we

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:43.300
may back off, but if the Iranian economy improves
over the next six months, then we'll be back.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:46.850
That was sort of his standard for what was
going on.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:53.470
So I think it's a very fluid situation. As
you know, 59 senators sponsored S-1881, or

00:07:53.470 --> 00:08:00.110
co-sponsored it, which was the sort of the
new sanctions bill sponsored by Senator Menendez.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:07.259
And there was a report over the weekend that
they felt they had 75 votes if it actually

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:12.330
came to the floor, which would have made it
veto-proof.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:17.490
A similar bill passed the House last summer
before the announcement of the interim agreement,

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:22.430
so that's a different kind of situation. But
still, I don't think there's any doubt that

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:29.080
there's substantial interest in this issue
and a substantial interest in the possibility

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:33.630
of additional sanctions.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:38.899
You all know -- you all know the issue. The
basic issue is what's -- what's Congress's

00:08:38.899 --> 00:08:49.140
role in this process while negotiations are
under way with regard to Iran? And I'm going

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:56.480
to walk through that, although I -- it's a
funny situation. I developed this position

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:02.290
thinking about this issue, talking to people
like Carl Levin and Dianne Feinstein and others

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:07.929
who I really respect on these kinds of issues
back in December.

00:09:07.929 --> 00:09:15.749
And at the time, my position, which was opposed
to the bill -- I was an outlier and a contrarian

00:09:15.749 --> 00:09:21.420
because all the momentum was in the direction
of -- of the sanctions. And then over the

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:28.930
course of the last several weeks, the momentum
seems to have shifted, and the push for new

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:34.480
sanctions, as I mentioned, even as recently
as this morning seems to have diminished.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:43.440
I feel good about that. I don't take responsibility
for it, but I can tell you that I and others

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:49.839
talked to other members of various caucuses
about this issue. And I have to say I think

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:57.250
one of the key moments was a week ago, on
a Thursday, when the Democratic caucus went

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:02.360
to the White House to meet with the president,
closed-door meeting, no press. There was very

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:07.550
little staff there. And it was a -- there
were a variety of issues. It wasn't -- there

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:12.560
wasn't any agenda, and the president was talking
about the minimum wage and the economy, middle

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:13.860
class, and all that.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:20.670
And one of the senators asked him about Iran,
and he gave -- he was the most powerful and

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:29.430
persuasive I have ever seen him on any subject,
on why it would be a mistake to move forward

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:36.580
with additional sanctions or even additional
sanctions triggered later, as were proposed

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.339
by the Menendez bill.

00:10:38.339 --> 00:10:42.970
I mean, I sat there and thought I don't know
how anybody can sit in this room and feel

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:50.420
that this is the right course, given his really
eloquent -- no notes, no teleprompter, no

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:56.239
anything, just sitting on a stool -- a really
powerful statement about, you know, the history

00:10:56.239 --> 00:10:58.529
of how we got here and what we should do.

00:10:58.529 --> 00:11:03.570
And I think that probably had something to
do with some of the steam coming out of this

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:11.959
issue, not that people were saying, We've
got to do this because we've got to be loyal

00:11:11.959 --> 00:11:18.040
to the president, but I think because he was
just downright persuasive. And I think that

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:23.800
was -- that does occasionally happen around
here, and I think this was a case where it

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:24.800
did.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.709
But I feel a little funny because I took a
position and the world changed, I still have

00:11:29.709 --> 00:11:35.779
the same position. Some of you remember Mort
Sahl from the '50s, the comedian. Mort Sahl

00:11:35.779 --> 00:11:41.730
once said if you maintain a consistent political
opinion in this country long enough, you will

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:47.790
eventually be tried for treason. And you know,
I sort of felt like I stayed the same and

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:54.369
everything sort of -- sort of shifted.

00:11:54.369 --> 00:12:02.470
Some important things about the sanctions.
We've had sanctions against Iran since 1979,

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:07.429
in one way, shape or form. But the sanctions
that have really bitten, the sanctions that

00:12:07.429 --> 00:12:13.050
have really changed the face of the Iranian
economy are those that went into effect around

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:19.959
2009, 2010 and involved the rest of the world.

00:12:19.959 --> 00:12:26.170
Iranian oil exports have gone from 2.5 million
barrels a day to 1. That's about a 60 percent

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:32.649
reduction. A hundred percent of that reduction
is because of reduced purchases by other countries,

00:12:32.649 --> 00:12:40.300
not by the U.S. The EU reduced their purchases
600,000 barrels a day -- China, Japan, India.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:45.819
So it's really important to realize that the
sanctions that are biting are the international

00:12:45.819 --> 00:12:48.619
coalition sanctions, not simply U.S. sanctions.

00:12:48.619 --> 00:12:53.720
Now, we've provoked a lot of those by pressure
on our allies. There's no question that we've

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:59.279
led it. But I think it's really important
to realize that unilateral sanctions from

00:12:59.279 --> 00:13:07.670
the U.S. aren't necessarily going to be the
way to change Iranian policy.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:13.509
The results have really been significant.
I mentioned the oil -- $100 billion to $30

00:13:13.509 --> 00:13:21.139
billion of revenue in -- in oil revenue, 50
percent inflation, 20 percent unemployment,

00:13:21.139 --> 00:13:26.899
5 percent contraction last year in the Iranian
GDP. This is -- this is the effect that it's

00:13:26.899 --> 00:13:29.139
had, and it's really been significant.

00:13:29.139 --> 00:13:34.069
And as you know, a new president was elected,
and the platform that he had was economic

00:13:34.069 --> 00:13:38.920
improvement. Sounds familiar. I mean, you
run for president, you say, We're going to

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:43.889
have more jobs, the economy's going to improve.
But he came to the realization that the sanctions

00:13:43.889 --> 00:13:49.989
were a significant part of the problem, and
he had a more open attitude toward trying

00:13:49.989 --> 00:13:53.730
to -- to modify them.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:59.170
So where are we? You all know. We've got a
six-month interim agreement with some very

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:05.149
significant -- I reread it this morning. There's
a good summary on WhiteHouse.gov. If you do

00:14:05.149 --> 00:14:11.519
WhiteHouse.gov summary of sanctions, you've
got the whole long list of -- of what they

00:14:11.519 --> 00:14:19.940
have to do and what we do in terms of the
-- the giving up of the -- easing up on about

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:25.179
$7 billion worth of value over the six months.

00:14:25.179 --> 00:14:30.040
And it's a pretty impressive piece of negotiation.
The value isn't all given on day one. It's

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:38.880
parceled out over a period of time. And the
sanctions -- the changes in the imposition

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:44.429
on the Iranian nuclear program are really
quite significant, talk about dilution of

00:14:44.429 --> 00:14:51.009
the 20 percent enriched, no more -- no more
5 percent enriched, restrictions on the Arak

00:14:51.009 --> 00:14:57.369
reactor, a lot of other detailed restrictions.
But probably more importantly, extraordinary

00:14:57.369 --> 00:15:03.730
new transparency and access. And that's the
key part of this, more than we've ever had

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:05.180
before.

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:11.759
And the real question is, Will it -- will
they do it? And because this is a project

00:15:11.759 --> 00:15:17.850
that is in at least two steps -- maybe more,
but at least two steps, we have an opportunity

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:23.049
to test the Iranians' good faith, to test
whether they are, in fact, going to live up

00:15:23.049 --> 00:15:26.990
to the commitments made in this interim agreement.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:34.429
And the terms of the interim agreement really
cause a pause in their ability to reach breakout

00:15:34.429 --> 00:15:44.619
in terms of a nuclear weapon. So it -- it's
a pretty solid piece of work in terms of the

00:15:44.619 --> 00:15:49.970
effects on the Iranians, and particularly
the transparency to see whether they're honoring

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:51.079
the commitment.

00:15:51.079 --> 00:15:55.959
It also is worth mentioning that the oil sanctions
don't change, that that's not part of the

00:15:55.959 --> 00:16:00.370
deal. The sanctions that have limited the
sale of oil are exactly the same. There's

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:03.680
not -- no opening up.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:10.709
Now, the proponents for the bill, S-1881,
make a plausible case. If sanctions have gotten

00:16:10.709 --> 00:16:16.669
us this far, won't more sanctions get us further?
If you strengthen the sanctions, it'll strengthen

00:16:16.669 --> 00:16:22.879
the president's hand. It'll give him a stronger
had to negotiation and we'll have a stronger

00:16:22.879 --> 00:16:24.769
agreement at the end.

00:16:24.769 --> 00:16:29.929
You know, that -- that makes sense. We'll
get a better deal. And besides, we can't trust

00:16:29.929 --> 00:16:36.750
the Iranians. And you know, Reagan said "Trust
but verify." I think, in this case, it's verify

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:41.720
but verify. I mean, there's a lot of history
here, and I think -- I don't think trust really

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:46.449
is necessarily part of the equation. It has
to be verify and verify.

00:16:46.449 --> 00:16:54.899
And I want to pause right now, just -- my
notes say "humility." Nobody knows how these

00:16:54.899 --> 00:17:00.290
things are going to play out. Nobody knows
what's going to happen over the next six months

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:06.910
or over the next couple of years. It's a very
unpredictable situation.

00:17:06.910 --> 00:17:14.270
So I'm standing here with great assurance,
giving you my position, but I don't guarantee

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:19.280
being right. And I don't think anybody can
take that position. I think when you're talking

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:27.260
about foreign policy, humility is an important
quality, to realize the challenges of predicting,

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:30.770
particularly when you're predicting what another
country and another culture, another political

00:17:30.770 --> 00:17:34.600
system is going to do.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:38.300
But the argument is, Let's strengthen the
president's hand. Let's get a better deal.

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:44.940
I don't -- I just don't think that works.
And I think the Congress -- and maybe this

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:49.690
is old news as of this morning, but the idea
of imposing new sanctions now I think is just

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:58.980
-- is not -- not useful. And I think it would
have exactly the opposite effect of that which

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:00.860
is desired.

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:07.290
One effect might well be the Iranians just
plain walk, that the hard-liners -- I mean,

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:12.100
I -- we -- I wrote a little piece with Carl
Levin, and we said that would -- that theory

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:18.220
would work if Iran was a politics-free zone.
But Iran has internal politics, just like

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:22.620
the U.S. or Great Britain or Germany or anywhere
else, and there are people in Iran who don't

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:27.570
like these conversations. They don't want
to give up any part of the nuclear program.

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:32.360
They -- they are very reluctant to give up
what they consider something important to

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:37.070
their national pride and perhaps national
survival.

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:44.070
So if we change the goalposts, if we say,
We're going to, by the way, put in new sanctions

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:50.310
-- and by the way, the interim agreement has
a specific provision that says, No new sanctions.

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:54.541
So we would be in violation of that interim
agreement, if we imposed new sanctions. It

00:18:54.541 --> 00:18:58.930
could be argued, Well, it's they trigger six
months. Does that count? I'm not sure that

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:03.010
-- that subtlety would translate very well
into Farsi.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:11.820
The headline in Tehran would be, U.S. Congress
imposes new sanctions. And my concern is that

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:18.081
would empower the hardliners to say, I told
you so. You can't trust the Americans. We're

00:19:18.081 --> 00:19:25.650
walking away from this. And this is the best
chance we've had in a decade or more to do

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:31.260
something about this very serious issue of
Iran -- of Iran having nuclear weapons.

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:40.380
So I worry about the hard-liners, even with
a delayed trigger, and that it would undercut

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:47.230
Rouhani and take away his ability to make
a deal. He's got -- he's got to play to his

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:56.820
political base, and of course, the Supreme
Leader in Iran has immense input on this.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:20:00.430
The second reason I think it's a bad idea
is that, again, to go back to what I mentioned,

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:05.180
this -- these sanctions work because of the
support of the international community. And

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:12.160
if we impose new sanctions and the international
-- the coalition, the partners that are involved

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:18.170
in the sanctions say, Well, that -- we wanted
this interim agreement, it tends to weaken

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:23.850
their support for the sanctions, which, ironically,
could end up leading to weaker sanctions in

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:29.660
the end. We would end up worse than we started
off because the real power of the sanctions

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:34.550
comes from the support of the international
community.

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:40.840
I think Congress has an important role. I
think Congress has an important role being

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:46.630
the sword in the sheath, if you will. But
you don't have to -- you don't have the bare

00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:53.970
the sword. Everybody knows that Iranians know
that Congress is ready to impose new sanctions,

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:56.990
is discussing it, is actively thinking about
it.

00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:01.140
But if they pass it and then the president
has to veto it, it just -- I think it makes

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:06.550
a very difficult situation for our negotiators,
and the Iranians say, Well, who does speak

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:12.040
for the U.S. government? We have this agreement
that went into effect last week, but it's

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:17.890
been abrogated, you know, two weeks later.
Who are we negotiating with here? Maybe we

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:21.970
should -- instead of talking to the president
or the secretary of state, we should talk

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:25.720
to Harry Reid and Bob Menendez or John Boehner.

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:30.750
I mean, I think it raises a legitimate question
about who speaks for the United States. So

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:39.670
I think Congress has an important role, but
I don't think it's to -- it's to come in and

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:46.900
raise a question about who speaks for the
-- for the United States government.

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:51.731
I think, finally, this is a pretty simple
question of risk analysis, for me. What's

00:21:51.731 --> 00:21:58.550
the up side and what's the down side? I see
very little up side to passing a bill of this

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:08.600
nature now. It may strengthen the president's
hand, maybe. That's the possible up side.

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:15.740
But the down side I view as enormous. If they
walk, if the talks end, if the sanction coalition

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:25.960
falls apart, that's -- we may have missed
a once-in-a-generation opportunity to deal

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:28.180
with this problem.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:34.950
And the final point is, there aren't many
options to deal with the problem of a potentially

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:42.060
nuclear-armed Iran. One is negotiation and
the other is military action. The third, I

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:49.240
suppose, is to tolerate a military -- a nuclear-armed
Iran, but nobody seems to want to consider

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:56.140
that as an option, and I don't think it should
be. But that leaves military force.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:23:01.560
And you know, maybe negotiations could start
again under different circumstances and we

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:07.930
could tighten the noose of sanctions, but
we've got this opportunity in front of us.

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:15.070
The agreements in the interim agreement are
pretty significant. They're not token. And

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:20.510
we have an opportunity to test the Iranians'
good faith, to test their ability to allow

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:27.040
access, to test their ability to actually
take concrete steps to step away from nuclear

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:33.650
arms. And I don't see much down side, particularly
since there's a pause in their development

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:40.680
of capability to break out, to allowing this
process to play out over the next six to twelve

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:41.680
months.

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:45.660
There are going to be ups and downs. There's
going to be posturing on both sides. There's

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:49.880
going to be difficult negotiations. And of
course, the ultimate question will be, What

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:55.220
does the final deal look like? And does it
-- is it something that we really want to

00:23:55.220 --> 00:24:05.130
-- is it good enough to satisfy us and our
allies that we've prevented this -- the nuclear

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:10.190
armament of Iran. But that's going to be part
of the discussion over the next six months.

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:19.780
So my view is, as I certainly made clear,
that we have an opportunity here and we shouldn't

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:25.990
let it slip away. Ronald Reagan said a very
interesting thing. He said, Peace is not the

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:31.650
absence of conflict. There will always be
conflict in the world. But peace is the ability

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:38.340
to cope with conflict by peaceful means. And
I think we're in a moment now where we have

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:44.230
an opportunity to cope with conflict by peaceable
means. And I certainly hope that we seize

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:49.460
it, and I hope that the Congress is able to
play a constructive role in this process.

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:54.070
So that's -- I hope I've given you some food
for thought, and now we'll have a conversation.

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:58.070
Thank you very much for giving me this opportunity.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:24:59.090
(APPLAUSE)

00:24:59.090 --> 00:25:06.100
BRENNAN: Senator, I should have led with you
went to University of Virginia Law school.

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:07.100
KING: Yes.

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:08.370
BRENNAN: Because I'm a UVA grad...

00:25:08.370 --> 00:25:09.370
(CROSSTALK)

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:10.370
KING: Good for you.

00:25:10.370 --> 00:25:11.840
BRENNAN: Key element there in your bio.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:16.080
I want to follow up on some of what you're
talking about. Just to clarify, sounds like

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:20.800
what you believe, momentum is slowing on the
Hill in terms of this threat to impose new

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:23.940
sanctions. Is this bill really on ice?

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:30.580
KING: Well, things change day to day, but
I would say, based on the story this morning,

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:36.710
based on comments I'm getting on the floor,
the momentum has slowed in terms of bringing

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:45.930
it forward. I don't see the sponsors withdrawing
it or anything like that. My guess is -- but

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:51.190
-- and you know, we're all going -- literally,
this was a Reuters report this morning that

00:25:51.190 --> 00:25:56.560
a new version is being drafted that doesn't
necessarily involve new sanctions, but will

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:01.210
involve some language, you know, keeping the
pressure on the administration.

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:08.830
So if I had to bet right now, I would think
that it probably won't come to the floor any

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:13.440
time soon, but I don't have that directly
from Harry Reid, but that's my impression.

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:19.450
BRENNAN: You talked about a change leading
to this probably happening around last week.

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:25.490
Last week's also when this deal went into
effect, last Monday. To a certain extent -- maybe

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:29.320
this is a cynical view or a cynical posture
to pose here.

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:30.670
KING: A member of the press cynical?

00:26:30.670 --> 00:26:37.390
BRENNAN: I'm a paid cynic. So when you look
at the timing here, hasn't Congress's role

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:43.120
really been tactical pressure for the administration
because these technical details have been

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:46.980
worked out in the past few weeks before the
agreement went into effect on Monday?

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:52.970
KING: I think the pressure has helped, for
sure. Somebody said that this is a bill that's

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:59.700
better threatened than passed. And I think,
to some extent, that was the case.

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:05.370
The momentum started to change, I think, when
people like Dianne Feinstein went to the floor

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:11.970
and made a very strong speech against it,
Carl Levin, who's, you know, been very strong

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:19.670
on Iran and these issues, and then some of
the people in this audience who've made statements,

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:24.210
Jessica Matthews's story in The New York Review
of Books.

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:27.720
I think a lot of people have been sort of
saying, Wait a minute. Slow down. What would

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:33.641
be the effect here? Because it -- at the beginning,
it was, you know, Let's go and -- and you

00:27:33.641 --> 00:27:38.680
know, they got 59 co-sponsors in a matter
of weeks, and then it kind of stopped.

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:43.420
BRENNAN: So you're on the Intelligence Committee.
You've received briefings on what's actually

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:48.420
in this deal. Does this extend Iran's breakout
time, the time it takes for them to actually

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:49.559
build a weapon?

00:27:49.559 --> 00:27:56.500
KING: I've got to be really careful and remember
what I know in what context and what I can

00:27:56.500 --> 00:28:05.200
talk about. But I think it's public knowledge
that the terms that -- that caused the dilution

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:12.050
of the 20 percent enriched, the freezing of
the 5 percent, in effect, the freezing of

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:19.260
the construction of the Arak reactor does
delay the breakout time to some extent.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:23.350
The other piece of intelligence -- and I've
seen both classified and unclassified, but

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:28.860
the unclassified is the intelligence community
does believe that additional sanctions now

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:36.900
would -- there's a -- can't remember if moderate
-- high likelihood that the Iranians would

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:45.100
walk. So there's an intelligence basis for
-- for that concern, because of internal politics

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:46.330
of -- in Iran.

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:50.320
BRENNAN: One of the other things you talked
about in your remarks there was not just the

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:56.310
Iranians walking but part of the alliance
breaking up. Is that code for Russia would

00:28:56.310 --> 00:29:00.140
walk, Russia would start violating these sanctions,
China would?

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:05.580
KING: That isn't -- that wasn't my intention.
I don't know -- there -- I don't know who

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:15.190
might break off, but it's not -- I think there's
some fragility, and I don't know who it would

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:20.190
be, but some of these countries that are backing
off of their purchases, it's at some cost

00:29:20.190 --> 00:29:27.620
to them. It isn't something that they were
thrilled to do. And so you know, I don't know

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:33.880
whether -- I couldn't say whether -- Russia
is less a player on the oil front because

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:39.721
they don't purchase much oil. The EU and China
and India are the biggest that changed that

00:29:39.721 --> 00:29:42.180
equation.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:48.540
The other piece -- and Mr. Pollack -- what's
his first name?

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:49.540
BRENNAN: Ken?

00:29:49.540 --> 00:29:54.770
KING: Ken Pollack wrote a good piece that,
in a sense, we have the high ground now. You

00:29:54.770 --> 00:30:02.670
know, we're negotiating, and if there's a
breach, it's on the Iranians. If we flip that

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:07.030
and impose new sanctions in violation of the
interim agreement, we lose the high ground

00:30:07.030 --> 00:30:14.830
and we -- and it gives our partners more of
a chance to walk. He argues that the most

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:19.300
powerful reason not to do this is the breakup
of the coalition.

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:24.309
BRENNAN: So with these six months, that time
that's been purchased, essentially, you say

00:30:24.309 --> 00:30:30.170
that has expanded the breakout time -- what's
gained over that? What happens at the end

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:31.170
of the six months?

00:30:31.170 --> 00:30:35.071
KING: Well, that's going to be a big question,
and I think one of the questions that has

00:30:35.071 --> 00:30:42.820
to be considered is what -- what is success?
What's the definition of success? Some of

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:51.830
our allies want success to be no nuclear capacity
at all, no enrichment capacity at all.

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:55.871
The indications from Iran is that they're
not going to accept that. So the question

00:30:55.871 --> 00:31:04.271
is what between zero and something is going
to be acceptable in the agreement, and there

00:31:04.271 --> 00:31:10.290
are going to be all kinds of other pieces
to it in terms of reactors and centrifuges

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:15.580
and parts and inspections and all those kinds
of things.

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:23.780
There's no certainty here. My position is
that we -- if we -- if we can have a pause,

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:31.510
a slowdown of the breakout period, and an
opportunity, a chance to get a deal, we ought

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:39.140
to take it because the other options are very
unattractive. And the -- the consensus seems

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:45.140
to be that if military force was used, the
best we could do is delay it. We couldn't

00:31:45.140 --> 00:31:51.690
necessarily eliminate the capacity, and if
we delay it, then we're back in the same box

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:53.390
at some period.

00:31:53.390 --> 00:32:01.730
Plus, if -- if we -- if there are air strikes,
whoever does them, it seems to me the chances

00:32:01.730 --> 00:32:07.811
that they would then forswear the development
of nuclear weapons go close to zero. They're

00:32:07.811 --> 00:32:15.040
going to say, We've got to have them. These
people are bombing us. And so we've accelerated

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:18.510
that intention.

00:32:18.510 --> 00:32:23.330
And right now, it's an open question. The
intelligence is unclear as to what the Supreme

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:27.770
Leader really wants to do here. There -- he's
made statements in the past saying this is

00:32:27.770 --> 00:32:31.920
inconsistent -- nuclear weapons are inconsistent
with Islamic law.

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:38.490
So I think, you know, in any kind of situation
like this, where you're making decisions,

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:46.600
you've got to say, Compared to what? And right
now, we've got a promising opportunity and

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:53.120
compared to what, meaning bombing, delay,
almost assured pursuit of a weapon after that

00:32:53.120 --> 00:32:57.780
-- to me, it's a good time to take a deep
breath and let it play out.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:01.560
BRENNAN: And when you were talking about breakout
time, you're talking about the capacity to

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:07.390
build a weapon, or the ability to weaponize?
So there seems to be two different definitions

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:08.390
there.

00:33:08.390 --> 00:33:12.480
KING: Well, I think it's capacity. I -- I
take a fairly conservative view on that, and

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:18.600
that if you -- if they have the -- if they
have the capacity to do it in a reasonably

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:23.970
short period of time, that's pretty close
to being able to do it, and that we really

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:26.590
need to be looking for an agreement that limits
that capacity.

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:30.559
BRENNAN: And so in these negotiations, you
think that -- that definition of breakout's

00:33:30.559 --> 00:33:32.750
been extended. By how much?

00:33:32.750 --> 00:33:40.840
KING: I couldn't say. Months? I think, as
I view it -- and again, there are probably

00:33:40.840 --> 00:33:46.110
people in this room who are more expert than
I am on this. But as I view the interim agreement,

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:49.059
it delays breakout by the period of the agreement.

00:33:49.059 --> 00:33:50.450
BRENNAN: About six months.

00:33:50.450 --> 00:34:01.100
KING: And -- but it could be two months either
way. But I don't think -- and I think this

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:09.279
is a legitimate question, is, is all this
talk just to get a delay to go toward breakout?

00:34:09.279 --> 00:34:13.329
You know, are we -- is it all just a sham?

00:34:13.329 --> 00:34:21.769
And the provisions of the interim agreement
I think are real and I think do have an effect,

00:34:21.769 --> 00:34:27.899
assuming they're -- they're done. I mean,
I think that's a big part of this is, is it

00:34:27.899 --> 00:34:32.649
going to be effectuated? Is it going to be
implemented? Are they going to meet their

00:34:32.649 --> 00:34:37.159
commitments? And do they allow the kind of
access that they're talking about, daily access

00:34:37.159 --> 00:34:42.730
instead of every two weeks, and you know,
instantaneous monitoring? If all of those

00:34:42.730 --> 00:34:49.609
things occur and we can verify, you know,
I think we're not -- we're not losing anything

00:34:49.609 --> 00:34:51.529
by providing this time.

00:34:51.529 --> 00:34:57.170
Now, you know, events could prove us -- could
prove me and others totally wrong, and there

00:34:57.170 --> 00:35:02.230
may be a lab under a mountain somewhere that
nobody knows about that, you know, is working

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:09.960
away at it. But given the facts as we know
it now, I think we need to take this time.

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:15.500
BRENNAN: And what level of transparency is
there with the Hill right now, with people

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:20.869
on the committees you sit on, in terms of
that follow-through? How regular will you...

00:35:20.869 --> 00:35:21.869
(CROSSTALK)

00:35:21.869 --> 00:35:26.989
KING: There are some Intelligence hearing
tomorrow on the -- it's the -- it's the annual

00:35:26.989 --> 00:35:31.359
worldwide threat assessment, which I know
this is going to be a major thrust. It's an

00:35:31.359 --> 00:35:38.440
open hearing. So we will talk about that,
undoubtedly. And I talked to Chairman Feinstein

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:44.319
about this. There are going to be closed hearings
on Iran, so we have a full briefing on what

00:35:44.319 --> 00:35:49.739
-- what we know, how we know it, and how transparent
the process is.

00:35:49.739 --> 00:35:54.030
And I think that's one of the most important
responsibilities that we have is to be sure

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:58.789
that that's the case, that it's not wishful
thinking, that we're not just hoping that

00:35:58.789 --> 00:36:04.080
they're doing it because as far as I'm concerned,
if it's not verifiable, then, you know, doesn't

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:10.890
count. That's got to be a place where I think
Congress keeps its thumb on the administration.

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:16.170
And as I mentioned, there's a -- Congress
has a role here. It's just a question of how

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:21.859
overt the role is. But I think the pressure
on the administration is important. The pressure

00:36:21.859 --> 00:36:28.319
for verification is important. And you know,
we're going to be continuing to ask the questions.

00:36:28.319 --> 00:36:34.130
BRENNAN: I do want to get to some questions
from the audience. One more before I do that,

00:36:34.130 --> 00:36:41.180
and that is -- and this is more of a policy
question. I do want to -- as to your personal

00:36:41.180 --> 00:36:46.619
views, but do you think any of this actually
leads to empowering the moderates in a way,

00:36:46.619 --> 00:36:51.250
the relative moderates in Iran, that could,
in theory, lead to a grand bargain? Is there

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:53.569
something more to be gained, or are we purely...

00:36:53.569 --> 00:36:55.200
KING: Grand bargain? I thought that was with
Boehner.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:56.200
(LAUGHTER)

00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:58.140
KING: I know what you mean.

00:36:58.140 --> 00:37:04.130
BRENNAN: On Syria and other issues that are
also of concern to the administration.

00:37:04.130 --> 00:37:09.619
KING: I wish I could say yes. I -- I don't
see this link to Syria. I think Syria's a

00:37:09.619 --> 00:37:16.160
separate issue. You were just there. Syria's
the most complex public policy issue I've

00:37:16.160 --> 00:37:22.930
ever looked at, with the possible exception
of health care. So I don't think there's necessarily

00:37:22.930 --> 00:37:25.200
a linkage there.

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:36.370
I do think -- you know, Iran is an ancient
civilization, a pretty modern country, and

00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:44.269
you know, we've been estranged from them since
'79 and no -- there's not going to be a normalization

00:37:44.269 --> 00:37:49.300
of relations overnight by any means, but this
could be a step toward opening up of that

00:37:49.300 --> 00:37:57.480
country somewhat more to the West and a moderation
of their politics. But I don't -- I don't

00:37:57.480 --> 00:38:03.970
see, you know, regime change any time soon
there, and that's an unrealistic expectation.

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:10.450
BRENNAN: Well, I do want to get to the audience
now. We have microphones. If you would just

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:12.450
say your name and affiliation and stand up.

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:13.829
KING: Somebody's going to...

00:38:13.829 --> 00:38:14.829
(CROSSTALK)

00:38:14.829 --> 00:38:15.980
BRENNAN: ... front row.

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:20.690
QUESTION: Thank you. Morning. Richard Downey
(ph) from Delphi Strategic Consulting. And

00:38:20.690 --> 00:38:23.269
thank you very much for interesting comments
and discussion.

00:38:23.269 --> 00:38:28.970
I wanted to ask you to clarify something you
just touched on briefly. You mentioned that

00:38:28.970 --> 00:38:36.670
the success of the sanctions was really due,
after 2009, 2010, to the -- the international

00:38:36.670 --> 00:38:42.420
community really following through on this.
And you also mentioned that the -- it's all

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:48.599
about verifying. Trust doesn't really work,
that we really have to ensure that the Iranians

00:38:48.599 --> 00:38:51.859
follow through on what they've promised.

00:38:51.859 --> 00:38:56.270
But when you addressed the Kenneth Pollack
article, you just sort of glanced by -- I

00:38:56.270 --> 00:39:03.670
wonder, do you believe that as we move through
this process that the -- that the international

00:39:03.670 --> 00:39:10.700
community will weaken its -- its strength
in terms of focusing on this? And can we -- can

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:16.430
we gather that same kind of power in sanctions
later on, if the Iranians do renege on what

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:17.430
they promised to do?

00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:21.359
KING: That's a very good question, and I think
there is concern that the -- that opening

00:39:21.359 --> 00:39:28.589
-- opening the door to the easing of the sanctions
can create a slippery slope and we're -- it's

00:39:28.589 --> 00:39:31.130
going to be very hard to put it back together.

00:39:31.130 --> 00:39:36.470
I think if you focus, though, on what exactly
the opening of the door is, it's pretty limited.

00:39:36.470 --> 00:39:43.549
It's not a change in the -- in the oil limitations,
for example. It's -- it's -- of the $7 billion

00:39:43.549 --> 00:39:49.460
of sanctions relief, $4.5 billion or $4.2
billion is the freeing up of Iranian frozen

00:39:49.460 --> 00:39:56.369
assets that -- you know, that they -- from
their oil from previous years. So it's not

00:39:56.369 --> 00:40:01.520
that there's this huge opening, although I
have heard that there's a lot of interest

00:40:01.520 --> 00:40:06.930
in Teheran, there are a lot of foreign companies
that are trying to get ready to do business,

00:40:06.930 --> 00:40:13.130
and those kinds of things. And I think that's
something that we have to keep an eye on.

00:40:13.130 --> 00:40:18.680
But if we -- it seems -- the question is,
what makes the international coalition sticking

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:24.339
together more or less likely? My sense is
that us imposing additional sanctions now

00:40:24.339 --> 00:40:32.329
in violation of the interim agreement, that
they, after all, helped negotiate, would undermine

00:40:32.329 --> 00:40:38.660
that more than the modest reductions in the
sanctions that are going to go over the six

00:40:38.660 --> 00:40:41.269
months. I mean, that's -- that's a -- that's
a judgment call.

00:40:41.269 --> 00:40:47.849
And I think it's a legitimate question, but
their commitment, I think, rests at least

00:40:47.849 --> 00:40:54.140
in part on their view that, Hey, we did what
you wanted us to do. It's had the effect you

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:59.821
wanted. We got to the table. They're making
some movement. Let's keep going. That -- that

00:40:59.821 --> 00:41:04.880
-- you know, and it -- you know, and it's
-- I can't speak for the Chinese or the Japanese

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:13.069
and what their motivations are, but that's
my sense, is that it's more likely to hold

00:41:13.069 --> 00:41:15.950
if we honor the interim agreement than the
contrary.

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:20.859
BRENNAN: But to follow on that, I mean, in
Davos last week, we were standing there with

00:41:20.859 --> 00:41:25.619
Secretary Kerry, and on another stage, Rouhani
standing up there saying Iran's open for business.

00:41:25.619 --> 00:41:29.109
Come look at what we got. Come see our oil
fields. That's a bet that six months from

00:41:29.109 --> 00:41:34.460
now, there's going to be an ability to invest
or that there are enough cracks to the regime

00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:35.460
to allow for it.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:43.249
KING: Well, listen, I want them to be motivated
to make a good deal. I mean, that's -- I mean,

00:41:43.249 --> 00:41:49.349
you got to declare -- sometimes, you got to
declare victory. I mean, if we get a solid,

00:41:49.349 --> 00:41:55.570
verifiable deal that solves this problem,
then we've achieved what we wanted to achieve.

00:41:55.570 --> 00:42:00.099
The purpose of the sanctions was to keep Iran
from getting a nuclear weapon, and that should

00:42:00.099 --> 00:42:01.839
be the -- that's the goal.

00:42:01.839 --> 00:42:08.180
BRENNAN: On this side of the room, do we have
questions? And here in the middle, can we

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:09.920
run a mic , second row.

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:13.829
KING: Don't have to only talk about Iran,
by the way. If you want to talk about the

00:42:13.829 --> 00:42:16.009
farm bill, I can do that, too.

00:42:16.009 --> 00:42:20.109
QUESTION: As a fellow property tax payer and
neighbor in the town of Brunswick...

00:42:20.109 --> 00:42:21.109
KING: Hey!

00:42:21.109 --> 00:42:22.109
QUESTION: ... I just want to welcome you to
(inaudible)

00:42:22.109 --> 00:42:23.109
KING: You're a great American.

00:42:23.109 --> 00:42:24.109
QUESTION: ... to the council.

00:42:24.109 --> 00:42:25.109
(LAUGHTER)

00:42:25.109 --> 00:42:30.440
QUESTION: Gordon Adams (ph), American University.
Couple of quickies. One is, you -- I wonder

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:34.420
what the advantage -- this seems to be a very
strong moment that your remarks suggest here,

00:42:34.420 --> 00:42:40.289
where both sides in this negotiation actually
are playing good cop/bad cop. On both sides

00:42:40.289 --> 00:42:44.140
of this, there is, Watch out for these conservatives
behind me be because they're going to derail

00:42:44.140 --> 00:42:48.479
this agreement if you give them half an opening.
On our side, watch out for these folks over

00:42:48.479 --> 00:42:51.650
here with sanctions in the Senate because
they're going to derail this negotiation.

00:42:51.650 --> 00:42:55.700
Does that make it more propitious or less
propitious to actually get a deal? There's

00:42:55.700 --> 00:42:57.749
a curious symmetry there.

00:42:57.749 --> 00:43:02.470
The other question was, I wanted to push back
a little bit on the question of other issues

00:43:02.470 --> 00:43:08.230
because there is this broader agenda with
Iran, which is support for terrorism and meddling

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:12.549
in Syria and meddling in Iraq and other issues
that are of importance to us.

00:43:12.549 --> 00:43:16.319
Could you be -- give us just a little bit
more on why you think it makes sense to sort

00:43:16.319 --> 00:43:19.180
this piece out separately from that broader
discussion.

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:24.950
KING: I didn't -- I didn't mean to imply that
it should be sorted out. I just -- I think

00:43:24.950 --> 00:43:29.510
what I -- I hope what I communicated was that
I'm not sure we're going to be able to join

00:43:29.510 --> 00:43:34.589
them. There's a difference. I'm not saying
we should explicitly -- if we can settle some

00:43:34.589 --> 00:43:40.979
of these other issues and do something about,
you know, Hezbollah and Hamas and -- and support

00:43:40.979 --> 00:43:48.589
for Syria, all the better, and the other -- export
of terrorism and those kinds of things.

00:43:48.589 --> 00:43:54.050
That -- that would be a great result. I'm
just not sure that that's achievable, given

00:43:54.050 --> 00:43:59.250
the scope of what's being discussed. Might
be possible. Part of that is going to depend

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:03.809
upon how the international partners feel about
those other issues and whether they're willing

00:44:03.809 --> 00:44:11.550
to stick with some level of sanctions in order
to achieve a larger -- a larger result.

00:44:11.550 --> 00:44:17.479
Your comment about both sides have the -- I
liken it to a used car deal, where there's

00:44:17.479 --> 00:44:21.339
always a guy upstairs. You know, you negotiate
with the salesman, and he says, Well, I'm

00:44:21.339 --> 00:44:25.519
not sure. I've got to check with the guy upstairs.
I've never been sure whether that person really

00:44:25.519 --> 00:44:30.470
exists. But that's a -- that's a common function
in any negotiation.

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:35.400
And I think, clearly, the president's hand
is strengthened by the fact that Congress

00:44:35.400 --> 00:44:43.740
is -- is behind the curtain here, and everybody
knows that they're prepared to move forward,

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:51.329
for example, if the -- the deal doesn't get
to the proper place, there's a lack of verification,

00:44:51.329 --> 00:44:55.890
I can tell you it's not going to take long
for -- for that bill to come to the floor.

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:58.950
I mean, if Harry Reid says it's coming to
the floor, it's going to come to the floor

00:44:58.950 --> 00:45:06.720
and it's going to get its -- going to get,
you know, solid support.

00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:13.579
But -- so I do think that the people in the
background do have an important role to play.

00:45:13.579 --> 00:45:19.509
I often have thought -- going back, you know,
to Vietnam and -- and you know, foreign policy

00:45:19.509 --> 00:45:24.500
in the last 50 years, one of the fundamental
mistakes we as Americans make is to assume

00:45:24.500 --> 00:45:30.960
that everybody else in the world thinks like
we do, that they -- and they -- and they don't.

00:45:30.960 --> 00:45:33.569
They have different cultures. They have different
values.

00:45:33.569 --> 00:45:38.269
And you know, I can remember in college hearing
speeches from the Bundys about this rational

00:45:38.269 --> 00:45:42.750
policy on Vietnam and how we were going to
squeeze them and ratchet it up, and they were

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:47.130
going to have to give in. And I remember George
Mitchell talking about Saddam and he was going

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:53.999
to have to give in because his economy shrunk
by .2 percent. That -- it just doesn't necessarily

00:45:53.999 --> 00:45:55.720
work that way.

00:45:55.720 --> 00:46:00.140
And so I think we have to understand and try
to put ourselves into the shoes of this other

00:46:00.140 --> 00:46:06.809
society in terms of what -- you know, what's
going to be -- what's going to be possible.

00:46:06.809 --> 00:46:14.471
But you know, this moment of a nuclear Iran
-- and one of the -- Jessica Matthews (ph)

00:46:14.471 --> 00:46:19.049
makes the point that usually, when one country
in a region goes nuclear, it -- they do it

00:46:19.049 --> 00:46:27.069
in clumps. And if Iran develops a weapon,
Saudi Arabia and Turkey and perhaps Egypt

00:46:27.069 --> 00:46:32.599
are going to feel like they have to. And then
we're -- you know, then we've got a -- you

00:46:32.599 --> 00:46:37.609
know, it's 1914 all over again with nuclear
weapons and the Middle East is the Balkans

00:46:37.609 --> 00:46:40.349
of this century.

00:46:40.349 --> 00:46:45.910
So this is a -- this is a really important
moment, and all the dynamics that are employed,

00:46:45.910 --> 00:46:52.430
but the key thing is we're talking for the
first time in 10 years, 12 years, and in reality,

00:46:52.430 --> 00:46:58.230
34 years, 35 years. And I just -- we can't
-- we just can't let this moment slip away.

00:46:58.230 --> 00:47:04.849
I used to teach about the Cuban missile crisis,
and to me, the elements of the Cuban missile

00:47:04.849 --> 00:47:14.960
crisis were four -- an existential threat,
a credible threat of force, some kind of sanction,

00:47:14.960 --> 00:47:22.349
which was the quarantine, and negotiation.
Put those together and you can solve the problem.

00:47:22.349 --> 00:47:25.440
And I think this situation is very similar.

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:29.309
And if it hadn't -- you know, if nobody -- anybody
studies the Cuban missile crisis, if they

00:47:29.309 --> 00:47:35.109
hadn't kept talking, you know, it would have
been a disaster, you know, there's no word

00:47:35.109 --> 00:47:44.049
for it. Catastrophe is too mild a word. And
these moments come and go in history. And

00:47:44.049 --> 00:47:51.049
we've got to seize this one, in my view -- not
naively or blindly or, you know, acquiescently,

00:47:51.049 --> 00:47:57.599
if that's a word, but realistically, with
our eyes open, understanding the risks, and

00:47:57.599 --> 00:47:59.940
understanding the importnace of verification.

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:03.589
BRENNAN: Question here in the front, and then
here on the side.

00:48:03.589 --> 00:48:13.069
QUESTION: Allen Went (ph), formerly with the
State Department. Senator, in the past, we

00:48:13.069 --> 00:48:20.160
have been concerned that IAEA inspection procedures
in Iran were simply inadequate to detect any

00:48:20.160 --> 00:48:26.749
violation of Iran's commitments. What's different
this time that satisfies us that the inspection

00:48:26.749 --> 00:48:31.310
procedures will be intrusive enough to detect
any Iranian violations?

00:48:31.310 --> 00:48:37.549
KING: Well, I don't -- I don't have a specific
sort of intelligence-based answer to that,

00:48:37.549 --> 00:48:41.589
except that we have a period to test that.
I mean, we're going to find out in a matter

00:48:41.589 --> 00:48:47.020
of weeks how much access they have and how
often they can get in, what the monitoring

00:48:47.020 --> 00:48:52.539
is going to be, what the real-time streaming
video monitoring is going to be, what facilities

00:48:52.539 --> 00:48:53.720
they get to.

00:48:53.720 --> 00:48:59.069
And I think this is a continuing process.
It's not now and in six months. I think it's

00:48:59.069 --> 00:49:07.010
-- there's a -- it's a -- it's a process whereby
we can try to determine, A, the level of verification,

00:49:07.010 --> 00:49:12.989
B, whether it's sufficiently rigorous, and
C, whether the Iranians are -- are playing

00:49:12.989 --> 00:49:18.130
straight with us. And at any time along that
process, we'll be able to say, Wait a minute.

00:49:18.130 --> 00:49:19.989
This isn't working.

00:49:19.989 --> 00:49:25.859
So I can't tell you today that they have 42
inspectors where they used to have 24. I don't

00:49:25.859 --> 00:49:32.170
know that. I do know that inspection is a
key part, maybe the most important part of

00:49:32.170 --> 00:49:33.269
the interim agreement.

00:49:33.269 --> 00:49:37.049
BRENNAN: Here on the side.

00:49:37.049 --> 00:49:41.990
QUESTION: I'm Morton Halperin (ph) from the
Open Society Foundations. Senator, as you

00:49:41.990 --> 00:49:46.609
know, the Armed Services Committee on which
you sit did a very important study of detainee

00:49:46.609 --> 00:49:51.810
treatment, and then that report was released
and helped educate the American people. Now

00:49:51.810 --> 00:49:56.950
the Intelligence Committee, on which you also
sit, has completed another report, and I wonder

00:49:56.950 --> 00:50:01.079
whether you support declassifying and releasing
that report.

00:50:01.079 --> 00:50:08.720
KING: I could give you the answer as of today.
Probably, but I'm still not fully convinced.

00:50:08.720 --> 00:50:14.880
There were some limitations with the Intelligence
Committee. This is a study done of torture,

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:22.289
basically, and what -- what we did in the
early part of the century. There were some

00:50:22.289 --> 00:50:28.230
limitations to that study. Nobody was interviewed.
It was all documentary. And you always get

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:32.779
a -- I'm not sure you get a fully accurate
picture when you're looking at documents and

00:50:32.779 --> 00:50:36.300
don't -- don't do interviews with the individuals
involved.

00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:40.799
I'm -- my judgment is going to be based upon
whether I think it's -- it will be helpful

00:50:40.799 --> 00:50:48.870
to the country and to the dialogue. I've seen
it. I've read it -- not all 6,000 pages, but

00:50:48.870 --> 00:50:57.040
I've read a good deal of the -- of the substance
of it. And my inclination is to vote to release

00:50:57.040 --> 00:51:02.680
it, but I'm reserving judgment. I'm sure we're
going to have a vigorous debate in the -- in

00:51:02.680 --> 00:51:03.680
the committee.

00:51:03.680 --> 00:51:09.420
Unfortunately, as you may know, the committee
broke up on partisan lines, and the Republican

00:51:09.420 --> 00:51:15.079
members essentially didn't -- they pulled
their staff back from participation. So it

00:51:15.079 --> 00:51:18.740
wasn't a true joint report, which I think
is unfortunate.

00:51:18.740 --> 00:51:22.489
So I'm going to -- I'm going to listen to
the debate. I realized a couple -- about two

00:51:22.489 --> 00:51:29.210
months ago in an Intelligence Committee meeting,
I'm the deciding vote. There are 15 members.

00:51:29.210 --> 00:51:33.910
There are 7 Democrats, 7 Republicans and me.
And we had an amendment on something, and

00:51:33.910 --> 00:51:40.400
I voted no, and I realized I'd swayed -- you
know, I'd decided which way it went. That

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:46.920
was -- that was a little disconcerting. I
killed an amendment by Jay Rockefeller and

00:51:46.920 --> 00:51:49.910
apologized, he said, That's all right.

00:51:49.910 --> 00:51:50.910
(LAUGHTER)

00:51:50.910 --> 00:51:59.440
So I am going to have a -- I take very seriously
this -- this vote, and it's one that I'm -- as

00:51:59.440 --> 00:52:06.309
I say, I'm leaning toward yes, but I'm not
fully there yet. And the decision for me is,

00:52:06.309 --> 00:52:11.719
will it -- will it contribute to the public
dialogue and understanding, or will it distort

00:52:11.719 --> 00:52:17.289
it and not be an accurate picture. That's
-- that's where I am. Thank you for the question.

00:52:17.289 --> 00:52:21.849
BRENNAN: We have time for probably two questions.
If you could keep them succinct, that'll help

00:52:21.849 --> 00:52:24.569
us. Here in front.

00:52:24.569 --> 00:52:31.440
QUESTION: Hi. I'm Stephen Colpack (ph) from
Lincolnville, Maine. I wanted to ask you...

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:33.400
KING: If you're from Lincolnville, Maine,
what the hell are you doing here?

00:52:33.400 --> 00:52:34.400
(LAUGHTER)

00:52:34.400 --> 00:52:35.400
QUESTION: I came -- I came to see...

00:52:35.400 --> 00:52:36.900
KING: Lincolnville, Maine, is one of the most
beautiful places on earth.

00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:40.160
QUESTION: I came to see you, Senator.

00:52:40.160 --> 00:52:41.160
(LAUGHTER)

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:48.180
QUESTION: Totally different subject. You mentioned
that Senator Snowe stepped down because she

00:52:48.180 --> 00:52:54.029
was frustrated by the partisan politics. We
were very proud to elect you as an independent

00:52:54.029 --> 00:52:59.219
from Maine, and you talked about just now
your -- how that's actually come in handy

00:52:59.219 --> 00:53:04.470
a couple of times. How's it going for you,
the bipartisanship?

00:53:04.470 --> 00:53:06.539
(LAUGHTER)

00:53:06.539 --> 00:53:15.579
KING: Well, first I should say I'm having
a great time. I mean, I just have to admit

00:53:15.579 --> 00:53:20.089
that. It's frustrating at times, but I told
my wife a few weeks ago I've been preparing

00:53:20.089 --> 00:53:21.990
for this job all my life, I just didn't know
it.

00:53:21.990 --> 00:53:31.039
And it is frustrating. I mean, but there have
been opportunities where we've been able to

00:53:31.039 --> 00:53:37.559
nudge people. And in fact, the highlight and
the lowlight of last year was the same time,

00:53:37.559 --> 00:53:43.499
the shutdown. The low point was the shutdown.
It was so ridiculous, so avoidable, so predictable,

00:53:43.499 --> 00:53:50.130
so you know, harmful. The highlight was we
formed this little group with Susan Collins,

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:57.140
14 of us, that actually negotiated a settlement.
It was bipartisan. We had 7 Republicans, 6

00:53:57.140 --> 00:54:03.160
Democrats and me. And we negotiated a settlement,
and Susan went to Mitch McConnell and said,

00:54:03.160 --> 00:54:07.579
We've got a deal, and if you and Harry don't
work this out and announce something by 3:00

00:54:07.579 --> 00:54:09.839
this afternoon, we're having a press conference.

00:54:09.839 --> 00:54:16.670
And lo and behold, they finalized their deal.
Now, I'm not prepared to take credit for it,

00:54:16.670 --> 00:54:21.089
but I feel like we did make a contribution
to moving that process forward. And here's

00:54:21.089 --> 00:54:26.719
something really interesting. After that happened
and it got out that we had this little group,

00:54:26.719 --> 00:54:32.710
we started getting applicants to join. We
started hearing from other senators saying,

00:54:32.710 --> 00:54:34.810
We'd like to join that group.

00:54:34.810 --> 00:54:38.839
And in fact, we were supposed to have a meeting
this morning, but it's been postponed because

00:54:38.839 --> 00:54:42.760
of an Armed Services Committee meeting. But
we're now going to tackle some of these issues

00:54:42.760 --> 00:54:49.719
of amendments, of process, and work -- and
unemployment insurance as trying to work from

00:54:49.719 --> 00:54:51.010
the center out.

00:54:51.010 --> 00:54:57.029
If that works, it will have all been worth
it, even the $6 million they spent to try

00:54:57.029 --> 00:55:04.799
to defeat me . And so you know, I feel -- it's
still a very partisan place, but the interesting

00:55:04.799 --> 00:55:11.209
thing is it's not partisan personally. The
relationships are there. It's very positive

00:55:11.209 --> 00:55:19.269
relationships. And so I'm feeling -- I'm fired
up. It's -- it's -- it's amazing.

00:55:19.269 --> 00:55:23.789
And particularly for somebody who was there
as a staff member 40 years ago, I keep expecting

00:55:23.789 --> 00:55:27.839
somebody to walk onto the floor of the United
States Senate and say, What the hell are you

00:55:27.839 --> 00:55:33.400
doing here? You know? People say, Good morning,
Senator, and I do this. It's an amazing experience.

00:55:33.400 --> 00:55:38.230
And I walk to work. I live east of the Capitol
and walk to work down Maryland Avenue, and

00:55:38.230 --> 00:55:42.489
the sun is rising and lighting up the dome
of the Capitol, and to look up and see that

00:55:42.489 --> 00:55:49.269
and say, That's where I get to go to work.
It's humbling. It's really an amazing opportunity.

00:55:49.269 --> 00:55:55.630
So I'm -- I'm having a good time. Mostly.
My wife, if she were here, would say, He loves

00:55:55.630 --> 00:55:56.630
it.

00:55:56.630 --> 00:55:57.630
(LAUGHTER)

00:55:57.630 --> 00:56:03.579
BRENNAN: Back of the room I think there was
a question. Here, can we run a mic?

00:56:03.579 --> 00:56:08.859
QUESTION: Hello, everybody. (inaudible) from
Voice of America. Senator, some opponent of

00:56:08.859 --> 00:56:17.410
Assad's regime claimed that the nuclear deal
with Iran caused America's silence against

00:56:17.410 --> 00:56:20.000
Iran's assistance for the survival of Assad.
How...

00:56:20.000 --> 00:56:21.609
KING: For the survival of what? I'm sorry.

00:56:21.609 --> 00:56:22.609
BRENNAN: Assad.

00:56:22.609 --> 00:56:23.609
QUESTION: Assad regime.

00:56:23.609 --> 00:56:24.609
BRENNAN: Bashar al Assad.

00:56:24.609 --> 00:56:30.140
QUESTION: How in your perspective nuclear
deal affect America's position towards Syria?

00:56:30.140 --> 00:56:37.339
KING: I haven't -- I haven't seen that. Reporter
Margaret would be a better person to respond

00:56:37.339 --> 00:56:42.880
because she just came back from Geneva, where
-- and was with Secretary Kerry. But I don't

00:56:42.880 --> 00:56:51.289
-- I haven't heard of any talk of, Hey, we're
-- we're working on this nuclear deal, therefore

00:56:51.289 --> 00:56:56.049
we're going to take the pressure off Assad.
I -- I -- now, you know, I'm not in these

00:56:56.049 --> 00:57:03.029
meetings, but I have no knowledge of that
being the case. We want -- our policy is to

00:57:03.029 --> 00:57:11.470
get Assad out, to have a transitional government
and to try to do something for the humanitarian

00:57:11.470 --> 00:57:12.940
catastrophe that's taking place in that country.

00:57:12.940 --> 00:57:18.319
I was there -- Carl Levin and I went in the
summer to Turkey and Jordan, to the refugee

00:57:18.319 --> 00:57:26.459
camps, and it's -- it's an awful, awful situation.
And what the Assad regime does in terms of

00:57:26.459 --> 00:57:33.210
how they -- their tactic of just bombing a
town down to rubble is inhumane in the extreme.

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:36.829
But I haven't -- in answer to your question,
I haven't seen any evidence that there's a

00:57:36.829 --> 00:57:42.420
linkage, that somehow, behind the scenes,
the deal is, We'll do the nuclear thing if

00:57:42.420 --> 00:57:45.594
you'll leave our guy, Assad, alone. If that's
what you're suggesting, I haven't seen any

00:57:45.594 --> 00:57:46.594
evidence of that.

00:57:46.594 --> 00:57:47.594
BRENNAN: So we've hit the witching hour here...

00:57:47.594 --> 00:57:48.594
KING: Thank you.

00:57:48.594 --> 00:57:49.594
BRENNAN: ... of 9:30. So I know there are
more questions, but we're going to have to

00:57:49.594 --> 00:57:50.594
leave it there. Just to remind you all, this
was on the record and...

00:57:50.594 --> 00:57:51.594
KING: Oh, I didn't know that!

00:57:51.594 --> 00:57:52.594
(LAUGHTER)

00:57:52.594 --> 00:57:53.594
BRENNAN: You didn't see me furiously scribbling
down notes?

00:57:53.594 --> 00:57:54.594
(LAUGHTER)

00:57:54.594 --> 00:57:55.594
BRENNAN: Thank you very much, Senator, for
your time.

00:57:55.594 --> 00:57:56.594
KING: Thank you, Margaret. A real pleasure.

00:57:56.594 --> 00:57:57.594
BRENNAN: Interesting conversation.

00:57:57.594 --> 00:57:57.595
(APPLAUSE)

